# Native menu UIA resource audit (offline)

This folder contains an offline parser/comparator for the 24-row fixture
`dxui-plain-menu-uia-resources-v1`. It does not launch tests, build, access a
repository, or edit source. Run it only after the current `native-uia-final-matrix`
reports have completed and been copied/frozen:

```powershell
py -3 .\compare-menu-resources.py `
  --reference C:\RedSalamander.Perf\evidence\i26-ui\menu-plain-uia-20260927\profiles-navigation-final `
  --current <completed-profile-root> `
  --out .\menu-resource-comparison.json
```

The parser requires exactly one MenuAccessibility log per Release, Debug and
ASan-Debug profile; all 24 unique `(rows, cycle, phase)` keys; no heap-walk
errors; exact open geometry 476x322@96; and the expected visited children
(12 or 128). It reports warm cycles 1–3 as raw deltas and means, retaining
all per-cycle observations. It does not convert private/working-set deltas to
heap allocation claims. ASan heap-walker zero deltas are retained as recorded,
not interpreted as zero native allocation.

The old matching profile tree passed the parser's self-check. It has 24
observations/profile, 476x322@96 open geometry, and exact child counts. Derived
warm-open heap / process-private means (bytes) were:

| Profile | 12 rows: heap / private | 128 rows: heap / private |
|---|---:|---:|
| Release | 273,206 / 749,568 | 619,450 / 1,138,688 |
| Debug | 323,005 / 714,069 | 1,088,176 / 1,279,317 |
| ASan-Debug | 0 / 13,220,523 | 0 / 14,072,491 |

The ASan heap walk is not a usable allocation counter in this fixture; its
reported zero is preserved only as raw behavior. Old profile source hashes are
in the referenced tree's `source-hashes.json`; the menu test source hash there
is `F95BC52DFCC0DB98C073A74E317A93DE83264E3C23AF72E2AED92E17C200312A`.

## Separate commonComplexUI repeat context

The completed Release baseline and two repeat receipts are in
`native-uia-lifetime-baseline-20260928` and
`native-uia-lifetime-perf-repeat-20260928`. They share machine SINON, Ryzen 9
9950X3D, 96 DPI, fixture `dxui-complex-ui-v2`, WARP, 83 controls, 1,000 rows,
40 frames × 5 rounds. The repeat-1 comparison is `advice-required`; repeat-2
is `within-noise-budget`. Both repeats use the same candidate fingerprint and
executable; the baseline fingerprint/executable differs. This is meaningful
repeat-variation context, not a causal estimate for native UIA: the benchmark
runs offscreen and never attaches an Automation provider. Keep the original
reports and both repeats; do not replace the baseline or infer FPS from UIA
resource rows. The full-suite comparison is unpaired and remains a separate
gate.

## Current frozen-input attestation

The final native UIA matrix has completed. Its four changed source inputs are `src/Controls/DxUi.Accessibility.cpp`, `src/Controls/DxUi.Controls.cpp`, `Tests/Controls/DxUiTests.Accessibility.cpp`, and `Tests/Controls/DxUiTests.Tooltip.cpp`; each live qualified-checkout byte hash matches `frozen-inputs.json`. `Tests/Controls/DxUiTests.Menu.cpp` is not in that changed-input list, is clean in `git diff`, and its SHA-256 matches the old source receipt. The current performance receipts report source commit `3022541ec86c28cfeef5e16c4c8224b5de73be5d` with `sourceDirty:true`. Thus the menu fixture source is unchanged across the two measurements, but the run remains a dirty-source local qualification rather than exact-head CI.
## Completed final-matrix comparison (2026-09-28)

The completed native matrix reports all 18 x64 suites passing in Release,
Debug, and ASan Debug. Each selected MenuAccessibility log has the exact 24
observations. The menu test source was not modified in the qualified checkout:
`git diff -- Tests/Controls/DxUiTests.Menu.cpp` was empty and the file SHA-256
was `F95BC52DFCC0DB98C073A74E317A93DE83264E3C23AF72E2AED92E17C200312A`,
matching the old source receipt. The recorded run commit was
`3022541ec86c28cfeef5e16c4c8224b5de73be5d`; changed frozen inputs were Accessibility and Controls production code plus the Accessibility and Tooltip test files. The selected
old and current MenuAccessibility test reports point to fresh performance
receipts reporting the same `SINON` / Ryzen 9 9950X3D host at 96 DPI. Both
fixtures retain 476x322 open geometry and exactly 12/128 visited children for
all three warm cycles.

Open heap busy-byte mean deltas (old → current) were Release 12 rows
273,206→271,387 (-1,819 B) and 128 rows 619,450→616,136 (-3,314 B); Debug
323,005→322,485 (-520 B) and 1,088,176→1,087,123 (-1,053 B). These are small
negative shifts (about 0.1–0.7%). ASan heap busy deltas are zero in both
fixtures and remain uninterpretable. Open process-private mean deltas were
Release +40,960 B at 12 rows and -806,912 B at 128; Debug +4,096 B and
-47,787 B; ASan -1,348,949 B and -1,236,992 B. These three warm samples show
no consistent direction; they are not a performance qualification.

A small process-handle observation is retained rather than averaged away:
after close, the 12-row current samples were +0,+1,+1 handles in Release and
+0,+0,+1 in Debug, versus all zero in the old run. At 128 rows both runs had
+0,+0,+5 in Release and +0,+0,+5 in Debug; ASan also matched (0,0,0 for 12;
0,0,5 for 128). This is an aggregate process counter, not an object count or
proof of a UIA provider leak; only three cycles were measured. Keep the rows
and review whether a repeated targeted observation is warranted.

The offline `Tools/compare_performance.py` comparisons use the exact fresh
performance report named by each selected `Accessibility-x64-<profile>.json`
receipt, against the retained native lifetime baseline. They preserve the
comparator's exit/status:

| Profile | Status | Specific flags |
|---|---|---|
| Release | advice-required | dirty private peak +2.50%; dirty working set and peak +2.55% |
| Debug | advice-required | clean FPS -8.88%, frame P50 +11.59%, preparation/compose tails and process memory over their thresholds; dirty memory also exceeds 2% |
| ASan-Debug | within-noise-budget | no flagged metric |

The Release/Debug advice flags remain open for the full commonComplexUI gate;
they are not attributed to UIA lifetime code. This benchmark is the offscreen
WARP complex-UI fixture and does not attach an Automation provider. Separately,
the same-binary Release repeats against their own baseline ranged from
advice-required on repeat 1 to within-noise-budget on repeat 2, reinforcing
that those full-suite flags require confirmation and causal investigation,
not automatic acceptance or dismissal. They remain unpaired to this UIA
resource microfixture.

Run-provenance limit: current profile receipts report `sourceDirty:true` at
`3022541ec86c28cfeef5e16c4c8224b5de73be5d`. The frozen four-file source
manifest and independently rechecked bytes establish the tested modified
inputs; the unchanged menu test file is tracked and matches the old receipt.
This remains a dirty-source local run, not exact-head CI.

The same-binary Release repeat-1 comparison's flags were clean private bytes
(+5.15%), private peak (+5.55%), working set (+2.05%) and dirty working-set
peak (+2.02%); repeat 2 had none. Both reuse the same candidate executable
and source fingerprint, while the retained baseline fingerprint differs.



